# AWS CLI — Command Line Interface

## The Problem with the AWS UI

From Day 0 to Day 9, every resource (VPC, EC2, S3, etc.) was created using the **AWS Management Console (UI)**. While the UI is great for learning, it has a critical limitation:

> **The AWS UI is not automation-friendly.**

As a DevOps engineer, common daily requests include:
- Create 10 VPCs with specific configurations
- Provision 20 S3 buckets
- Launch 15 EC2 instances across multiple regions

Doing each of these tasks manually through the UI — clicking through pages, filling forms — is time-consuming, error-prone, and impossible to scale. This is the core problem that **automation tools** solve.

---

## What is an API?

**API** stands for **Application Programming Interface**.

Instead of interacting with AWS through a browser (UI), an API allows programs to interact with AWS **programmatically** — by sending structured HTTP requests directly to AWS endpoints.

**Conceptual example — creating an S3 bucket via API:**

```
POST https://api.aws.com/s3/create
Body: { "bucket_name": "my-bucket", "versioning": true }
```

AWS receives this request, creates the bucket, and returns the result as JSON.

Using APIs, any task that takes minutes in the UI can be scripted and executed in seconds — and repeated reliably thousands of times.

---

## Infrastructure Automation Tools on AWS

AWS exposes its API and several tools have been built on top of it to make automation accessible:

| Tool | Category | Best For |
|---|---|---|
| **AWS CLI** | Command Line | Quick tasks, scripting, day-to-day ops |
| **CloudFormation (CFT)** | IaC | AWS-native declarative infrastructure stacks |
| **Terraform** | IaC | Multi-cloud declarative infrastructure |
| **AWS CDK** | IaC (code-first) | Infrastructure defined in programming languages |

> **IaC** = Infrastructure as Code — defining and managing infrastructure through code/configuration files rather than manual processes.

All of these tools ultimately translate their inputs into **AWS API calls**. The difference is the abstraction layer they provide and the use cases they best serve.

---

## What is AWS CLI?

**AWS CLI (Command Line Interface)** is a Python-based utility developed and maintained by AWS itself. It acts as an abstraction layer between the user and the AWS API.

```
User Command  →  AWS CLI  →  AWS API Call  →  AWS Resource Created
```

- No need to write Python code or understand HTTP request formatting.
- Simply install the CLI, read the documentation, and pass commands with parameters.
- AWS CLI translates those commands into the correct API calls automatically.

**How it simplifies API interaction:**

Instead of writing a Python script with imports, HTTP methods, and JSON payloads to create an S3 bucket, the CLI reduces it to:

```bash
aws s3 mb s3://my-new-bucket
```

---

## When to Use CLI vs IaC Tools

| Scenario | Recommended Tool |
|---|---|
| Quick lookup — list S3 buckets, check EC2 status | **AWS CLI** |
| One-off resource creation or deletion | **AWS CLI** |
| Creating a full infrastructure stack (VPC + subnets + EC2 + LB) | **Terraform / CloudFormation** |
| Reproducible, version-controlled infrastructure | **Terraform / CloudFormation** |
| Reviewing infrastructure changes in a PR/code review | **Terraform / CloudFormation** |

> **Rule of thumb:** Use CLI for fast, simple, interactive tasks. Use IaC tools when building or managing complex, repeatable infrastructure.

---

## Practical Walkthrough

### Step 1: Install AWS CLI

> **Windows users:** Install [Oracle VirtualBox](https://www.virtualbox.org/) with a Linux VM, or use [Git Bash](https://git-scm.com/downloads) as a terminal alternative.

Go to the official AWS CLI documentation:
```
https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/userguide/getting-started-install.html
```

Select the installer for your OS and follow the instructions.

**macOS (command line installer):**

```bash
curl "https://awscli.amazonaws.com/AWSCLIV2.pkg" -o "AWSCLIV2.pkg"
sudo installer -pkg AWSCLIV2.pkg -target /
```

**Verify installation:**

```bash
aws --version
# Expected output: aws-cli/2.x.x Python/3.x.x ...
```

> Always install **AWS CLI v2** (version starts with `2.`). AWS CLI v1 is outdated.

---

### Step 2: Create an Access Key

AWS CLI needs credentials to authenticate to your AWS account — analogous to a username and password for UI login.

1. Log in to the **AWS Console**.
2. Click on your account name (top right) → **Security Credentials**.
3. Scroll down to **Access Keys** → **Create Access Key**.
4. If using the root account, acknowledge the warning → **Proceed**.
5. Copy the **Access Key ID** and **Secret Access Key**.

> ⚠️ **Never share your Secret Access Key.** Anyone with this key can access your AWS account. Delete unused access keys immediately after use.

> In organisations, always use **IAM user credentials** (not root) to generate access keys.

---

### Step 3: Configure AWS CLI

Run the configuration command and provide the credentials:

```bash
aws configure
```

You will be prompted for:

```
AWS Access Key ID [None]: <your-access-key-id>
AWS Secret Access Key [None]: <your-secret-access-key>
Default region name [None]: us-east-1
Default output format [None]: json
```

| Field | Description |
|---|---|
| Access Key ID | From Step 2 |
| Secret Access Key | From Step 2 |
| Default region | The AWS region to target by default (e.g., `us-east-1`) |
| Output format | `json` is recommended — structured, readable output |

---

### Step 4: Verify Configuration

Run a simple command to confirm the CLI is authenticated and talking to the correct account:

```bash
aws s3 ls
```

This should return a list of all S3 buckets in your account. If the output matches what you see in the AWS Console, the configuration is working correctly.

---

### Step 5: List S3 Buckets

```bash
aws s3 ls
```

**Example output:**

```
2024-06-06 10:22:34 app1-payments-prod-example.com
```

This confirms the CLI is connected to the correct AWS account and region.

---

### Step 6: Create an EC2 Instance via CLI

The following command creates an EC2 instance using the CLI. Replace the placeholder values with actual IDs from your AWS account:

```bash
aws ec2 run-instances \
  --image-id ami-0c02fb55956c7d316 \
  --instance-type t2.micro \
  --subnet-id subnet-xxxxxxxx \
  --security-group-ids sg-xxxxxxxx \
  --key-name your-key-pair-name \
  --count 1
```

| Parameter | Description |
|---|---|
| `--image-id` | AMI ID of the OS image (Ubuntu, Amazon Linux, etc.) |
| `--instance-type` | Instance size (e.g., `t2.micro` for Free Tier) |
| `--subnet-id` | Subnet where the instance will be placed |
| `--security-group-ids` | Security Group to attach |
| `--key-name` | Name of the key pair for SSH access |
| `--count` | Number of instances to launch |

**Output** (abbreviated JSON):

```json
{
  "Instances": [
    {
      "InstanceId": "i-0abcd1234efgh5678",
      "PrivateIpAddress": "172.31.103.118",
      "State": { "Name": "pending" },
      ...
    }
  ]
}
```

Verify in the AWS Console under **EC2 → Instances** — the private IP in the JSON output should match the instance shown.

**Error handling example:**

If a required parameter like `--image-id` is missing, the CLI returns a clear error:

```
An error occurred (MissingParameter) when calling the RunInstances operation:
The request must contain the parameter ImageId
```

The error message directly identifies the missing field — read it, check the docs, and correct the command.

---

## How to Read the AWS CLI Reference Docs

The AWS CLI reference documentation covers every available command, parameter, and option.

**Finding a command:**

1. Search for `AWS CLI reference <service>` in a browser (e.g., `AWS CLI reference S3`).
2. Navigate to the service documentation page.
3. Review the available commands in the **Synopsis** section.

**Common S3 CLI commands:**

| Command | Description |
|---|---|
| `aws s3 ls` | List all S3 buckets |
| `aws s3 mb s3://bucket-name` | Create (make) a new bucket |
| `aws s3 cp file.txt s3://bucket-name/` | Copy a file to a bucket |
| `aws s3 rm s3://bucket-name/file.txt` | Remove an object from a bucket |
| `aws s3 sync ./local-dir s3://bucket-name/` | Sync a local directory to a bucket |

**Common EC2 CLI commands:**

| Command | Description |
|---|---|
| `aws ec2 describe-instances` | List all EC2 instances |
| `aws ec2 run-instances ...` | Launch a new EC2 instance |
| `aws ec2 stop-instances --instance-ids i-xxxx` | Stop an instance |
| `aws ec2 terminate-instances --instance-ids i-xxxx` | Terminate an instance |

> For any unfamiliar command, look it up in the [AWS CLI Command Reference](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/reference/) — each command page lists all parameters with descriptions and example usage.

---

## Key Takeaways

- The **AWS UI is not automation-friendly** — using it for repeated or large-scale infrastructure tasks is slow and error-prone.
- **APIs** allow programmatic interaction with AWS — tools like the AWS CLI, Terraform, and CloudFormation are built on top of these APIs.
- **AWS CLI** is a Python utility that translates human-readable commands into AWS API calls — no programming knowledge required.
- **Access Keys** (Access Key ID + Secret Access Key) are the CLI equivalent of a username/password — never share them.
- Use `aws configure` to authenticate the CLI to an AWS account.
- The **AWS CLI reference documentation** is the single source of truth for all commands and parameters.
- **Use CLI for:** quick lookups, simple one-off tasks, day-to-day operations.
- **Use IaC tools (Terraform/CloudFormation) for:** creating full infrastructure stacks that need to be version-controlled and repeatable.

---
